      <div>
        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Isandlwana</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">22 January 1879</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The British camp below the hill of Isandlwana in Zululand, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, some 11 km from the valley where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> army was found</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a>, with Colonel Henry Pulleine commanding the camp and Colonel Anthony Durnford leading a part of it: approximately 1,800 British, colonial and native troops with approximately 350 civilians, of whom over 1,300 troops were left in the camp. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>, with the army commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntshingwayo-khoza">Ntshingwayo</a> kaMahole and Mavumengwana kaNdlela Ntuli: some 20,000 warriors</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> victory. Over 1,300 troops of the British force were killed, including Pulleine and Durnford, and the first British invasion of Zululand collapsed. Zulu dead are put at 1,000 to 3,000 in all.</span></div>
        </div>
